Z||000000||Patrick's Dad||01-10-2001||10:37 PM||||How many of you actually remember records? Or own a turntable?<P>I placed a bid on eBay (for something photographic), and, while I'm waiting, I got this idea to run down to the basement and bring up one of the boxes of albums I have in storage (We still haven't completely unpacked). I can't believe some of the stuff I paid money for. On the other hand, some of it would probably do well on the aforementioned auction site....<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000001||My7Fieros||01-10-2001||11:44 PM||||Just for the record (no pun intended), Im 28 years old..so records were around and the primary source for music until the early 80s (cassettes), then the mid 80s (cd's)...I currently own somewhere around 400+ Lps and around 200 45's. I have an old Garrard turntable that my dad bought in 1975...not only does it still work, but sounds GREAT!<BR>I love my LPs...I still try to buy them whenever I come across them.||||reg||14
Z||000002||bHooper||01-11-2001||09:46 AM||||i own about 100-150 albums, and no turn table!<P>about 250 cassette's and the only cassette player is in my pontiac, and jeep.<P>and 300 cd's, and i have about 7 cd players (counting computers, and playstations)!<P>some thing's do sound best on vinyl.  i have 3 copy's of Black Sabath's first album, an original vinyl one, a cassette and a CD.  in my opinion the album is the best one to listen too.  sure the cd is clear, and will "last forever", but there is some music that is better with a bit of static! <P>on the other hand, i couldn't imagine listening to Enigma or Pink Floyd on anything but CD... you need the clarity.<P>and of course, Frank sounds good no matter what the format!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||6
Z||000003||Cheever2||01-11-2001||10:56 AM||||No turntable here either.  Been wanting to replace the last one for years.<BR>Some of my record albums are:<BR>Stan-the-Man's Hit Record - no, he doesn't sing.  He gives batting tips.  Got it when I was a kid, around 1963.<BR>Carole King - Tapestry<BR>American Graffiti Soundtrack<BR>John Denver - Greatest Hits<BR>Linda Ronstadt - Hasten Down the Wind, and Simple Dreams<BR>Father Guido Sarducci - Live at St. Douglas Convent<BR>Marshall Tucker Band - Greatest Hits (closest I ever got to Country)<BR>Grover Washington Jr. - Feels So Good<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000004||Oreif||01-11-2001||11:03 AM||||I am a music nut, I have over 300 albums, 200 cassettes and over 1000 CD's! (yes I have a truntable)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||01-11-2001||12:29 PM||||Patrick's Dad,<P>I think you would be surprised.  The alt/indie/modern rock scene revolves around vinyl.  Here in Cincy there are several new vinyl only stores.  In fact just a couple of years ago, a major band released a vinyl only release that got national attention.  <P>Yes, I own a turntable... but am still waiting to purchase a good vacuum tube amp and pair of NHT reference speakers... of course I also have a separate home theatre system.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
